      "sequence_position": 8,
      "problem": "Prove that $11n+3$ is odd provided that $n$ is an even integer.\n\nLet $n$ be an even integer. Then, $n=\\,${{field-1}} for some integer $a.$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}11n+3 & ={{field-2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $b=$ {{field-3}}.  Since $a$ is {{field-4}}, we have that $b$ is {{field-5}}.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}11n+3 & ={{field-6}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is one larger than a multiple of $2.$ Therefore, $11n+3$ is {{field-7}}.",
      "worked_solution": "Recall the following:\n\n- If an integer $n$ is even, then there exists an integer $p$ such that $n=2p.$\n\n- If an integer $n$ is odd, then there exists an integer $q$ such that $n=2q+1.$\n\nIn this case, we need to show the following:\n\n$$\nn\\text{ is even}\\,⇒\\,11n+3\\text{ is odd}\n$$\n\nWe start by using the fact that $n$ is even.\n\nLet $n$ be an even integer. Then, $n=2a$ for some integer $a.$\n\nThe idea is to substitute $n=2a$ into our expression $11n+3$ and show it is odd by writing it as a multiple of $2,$ plus $1.$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}11n+3 & =11(2a)+3 \\\\  & =22a+3 \\\\  & =(22a+2)+1 \\\\  & =2{{(11a+1)}_{⏟}}_{b}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if we define another integer $b=11a+1$ (highlighted above), we have that $11n+3=2b+1,$ which shows that $11n+3$ is indeed one more than a multiple of $2.$\n\nLet $b=2a+1.$ Since $a$ is an integer, we have that $b$ is an integer.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}11n+3 & =2{{(2a+1)}_{⏟}}_{b}+1 \\\\  & =2b+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is one larger than a multiple of $2.$ \n\nFinally, we write our conclusion.\n\nTherefore, $11n+3$ is odd.",
